    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense from their perspective. but i'm still struggling to see how it's actually \"objective\" if the historical record is so messy and open to interpretation. isn't it a bit of a stretch to say they're just finding \"the truth\" when they're basically just picking whichever historical era supports their view of the law? it feels more like a way to avoid the hard work of modern moral reasoning.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "This is a common and strong critique, often arguing that the \"best meaning\" is always an opinion, making the \"objective\" claim weak. Some originalist advocates concede that a single, final \"truth\" is impossible, focusing instead on a \"best available understanding\" of the meaning at the time in question. They defend this by arguing that the political leaning of the judge is a bias, but the bias towards a particular *interpretive tool* (like the 18th-century public understanding) is a consistent, formal bias that should be favored over policy preference.",
      "source": "target",
